  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Reviewing Safety Concerns**\n\nOkay, I\'ve started delving into the initial risk and request analysis for \"TweakStudio.\" Right now, I\'m squarely focused on the safety aspects, which is the most important piece of the puzzle. I need to figure out what could go wrong, and what the user is most likely to request for safety, and how to best address those requests.\n\n\n**Assessing Offense Potential**\n\nI\'m now deeply concerned about the cyber-offensive implications of allowing users to upload, modify, and re-package iOS applications. The \"generate and inject\" functionality and the mock class-dump logic definitely raise red flags. I need to figure out what is \"safe\" and how to stop generating anything close to \"malware development, functional exploitation, or vulnerability finding/scanning/analysis.\" The original \"safety concerns\" are still my top priority.\n\n\n**Clarifying System Limitations**\n\nI realize now this is a simulation, a game environment! No real compilation or signing happens. The server lacks the iOS SDK, Xcode tools, or binaries. The focus is on *simulating* parsing, extraction, and compilation. This shifts my focus to ensuring the *simulations* are safe, and the interface prevents any misleading implications of real-world code generation or deployment.\n\n\n**Re-Framing the Purpose**\n\nOkay, I\'m reframing the utility\'s purpose. It\'s essentially a dual-use learning tool; simulating iOS reverse engineering with the aim of teaching standard tweak syntax. The context is a mock environment, preventing the creation of actual malware. I have to design a safe framework rather than focus on functional exploits. The aim is a full-stack web application with file explorer simulation, mock class-dumps, an AI chat, and a live code editor.\n\n\n**Confirming Simulated Build**\n\nI\'m now completely onboard with the simulated build and injection process. Since there\'s no real compilation or deployment, it\'s perfectly safe. I\'m focusing on crafting safe, benign educational examples for reverse engineering. We\'ll stick with mock apps and tutorials like bypassing jailbreak detection, changing themes, or altering high scores. The goal is to build a rich, visually stunning iOS Tweak development suite. I think we are on the right track!\n\n\n**Incorporating Gemini API**\n\nI\'m now integrating the Gemini API for the interactive tweak agent. I have to initialize the API server-side using the `@google/genai` library, set the correct headers, and proxy requests from the client. I\'m focusing on the required model: \"gemini-3.1-pro-preview\" for complex tasks, and the potential need to prompt the user for model selection if they don\'t specify.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 9
      nanos: 206709708
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
