  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Project Requirements**\n\nOkay, I\'m diving into the project requirements for \"TweakStudio - Conversational IPA Modder.\" The core challenge I\'m seeing is the need for a full-stack architecture. Specifically, a backend is essential. I\'m focusing now on outlining the specific functions and features we\'ll need for this initial phase.\n\n\n**Defining Server Capabilities**\n\nI\'ve been mapping out the server functionalities in `server.ts`. It\'ll handle header parsing, interactive chat with Gemini (using `@google/genai`), and compiling tweaks. I am also simulating .dylib injection into a binary. I\'m focusing on features such as functional UI, logs, download links, action paths, and decompilation lists within the prototype.\nI\'ve also started a Risk & Request Analysis to understand the user\'s needs.\n\n\n**Assessing Safety Protocols**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on safety protocols, specifically for an educational iOS tweak environment. The core focus is on benign simulations; textbook examples only. I\'ve confirmed that the Gemini API key will stay server-side, and that we will correctly simulate files to avoid any risk. It\'s a non-severity task, and I\'m aiming for a polished user experience. I\'m also reviewing `metadata.json` for updates.\n\n\n\n**Refining IPA Analysis**\n\nI\'m now refining the IPA analysis. The primary goal is to handle `.ipa` uploads via a drag-and-drop zone. The backend will extract and simulate the parsing of the app\'s binary headers to provide context. The design will allow the backend to read real `.ipa` or `.zip` files, examine their contents, and list Mach-O binary names. I also plan to extract key header information and Info.plist properties, generating realistic classes and methods for user interaction, as well.\n\n\n**Designing Chat Interactions**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the chat interface\'s functionality. My plan involves a split-pane layout: a conversational AI chat on the left that talks to the server, and a live code editor on the right for displaying `Tweak.x` or `Tweak.swift`. I\'m focusing on conversational tweaking: user prompts will trigger the chat agent to explain and update code in the editor using standard Logos syntax. To achieve this, the backend AI will return structured JSON, or we can use tool/tag extraction for live code editor updates!\n\n\n**Implementing Build Features**\n\nI am now focusing on the \"Compile & Inject\" functionality. When users click this, I\'ll simulate the compilation process using clang, linking with MobileSubstrate, and injection via `optool` or `Azule`. The user will see terminal-like compiler logs and then receive a download link for the modified app. The AI can also see the decompiled classes and files of the IPA, so that it can suggest tailored hooks!\n\n\n**Integrating IPA Knowledge**\n\nI\'ve made significant progress on integrating the decompiled IPA\'s state into the system. The backend now generates a class structure (e.g., `AntiJailbreakController`, `AppDelegate`) and sends this to Gemini as context, so the chatbot can run commands and respond. Furthermore, the chat will allow custom commands for IPA research. I\'m focusing on the sleek design now. It\'s time to build out a stylish dark mode with cyberpunk/terminal accents. I plan on using a monospaced font in the code editor, with simulated syntax highlighting and an interactive visual tree of the decompiled IPAs.\n\n\n**Upgrading Application Architecture**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a complete overhaul to a full-stack architecture to meet the project\'s requirements. This demands updating `package.json` scripts and adapting the workflow for server-side operations and API endpoints. The plan requires implementing features to handle file uploads, class storage, simulated commands, and compilation. I\'m prioritizing the implementation of these API endpoints in the `server.ts` file, as the project needs a full-stack architecture.\n\n\n**Evaluating Upload Strategy**\n\nI\'m now determining the optimal strategy for handling file uploads. I\'m evaluating the need for `multer` and considering alternatives like base64 or binary parsing within Express. The goal is a robust solution that avoids unnecessary dependencies, given the potentially standard file sizes. I\'m leaning toward a JSON-based upload with `express.raw` to manage the payloads.\n\n\n**Planning File Uploads**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the file upload process, specifically for `.ipa` files. The goal is a fast and responsive upload experience. I\'m considering client-side mock file parsing with backend extraction, or a robust server-side processing flow. I\'m choosing the standard Express JSON/raw parsers, with a 100MB limit, to handle the file payloads efficiently.\n\nI\'m also considering the app architecture, starting with `metadata.json` updates and defining types in `/src/types.ts`.\n\n\n**Refining Command Line Features**\n\nI\'m now implementing the command-line features within the chat interface, to provide detailed IPA research capabilities. I\'ll include commands to `class-dump` the structure of specific classes, `grep` for specific strings or keywords, and `strings` for binary string references. I\'m focusing on user-friendly explanations of how these commands work, in addition to simulating the output from these commands to help the user understand the context of their query.\n\n\n**Enhancing AI Chat Functionality**\n\nI\'m making progress on the `/api/chat` endpoint. It will allow the bot to respond directly within the UI. The Gemini API can handle app context, tweak code and user queries. It will return a JSON object with explanations, updated `Tweak.x` code, and optional commands. My focus is now on simulating the compile process by using generated files and merging them into a new download-able `.ipa`! The components are coming together nicely!\n\n\n**Designing UI Components**\n\nI\'m now building out the core UI components. I\'ve created a drag-and-drop zone with analysis details, an interactive tree for decompiled headers, a real-time chat with a security researcher bot, a custom code editor, and a compiling console with realistic logs. The main dashboard is also coming together, combining these panes in a split layout. My focus is now on `metadata.json` updates.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 23
      nanos: 58668585
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
