    thinking: "**Analyzing the Conflict**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed the discrepancy: the chatbot mentions the YouTube binary because `App.tsx` automatically loads a default YouTube `.ipa` file on component mount. This overrides the user\'s uploaded `.ipa` file unless specifically handled. I will explore how to integrate both files.\n\n\n**Eliminating Default Behaviors**\n\nI\'m now focused on removing all traces of default behavior to achieve a completely clean state. The \"YouTube.ipa\" preset and the templates dropdown are the first targets; I\'ll ensure these are entirely eliminated from the selector, as per the user\'s specific request. Instead, it\'ll show a fresh upload/drag-and-drop experience. The new logic is in place.\n\n\n**Refining Dynamic Metadata**\n\nI\'m now completely restructuring the metadata generation. Instead of mocks, the backend will derive metadata dynamically from the uploaded file\'s name. I\'m exploring intelligent parsing of the filename to generate classes, which will improve the chatbot\'s focus. I am now evaluating the feasibility of extracting class details from the file, allowing user input, or even Gemini-powered generation. I will check `IpaSelector.tsx` for file upload implementation details.\n\n\n**Implementing File Handling**\n\nI\'ve got the drag-and-drop file upload logic refined. Now I\'m ensuring we capture the dropped file for parsing. I\'m focusing on ensuring it is a `.ipa` file before proceeding. The goal here is swift response: I am considering parsing the file directly if possible, or letting the backend handle the full `.ipa` analysis. I am evaluating different file upload strategies to make the analysis as robust as possible.\n\n\n**Deconstructing Upload Process**\n\nI\'m now determining the most effective method for handling file uploads. I\'m considering several options: utilizing Node\'s built-in `zlib`, using the shell `unzip` command, or developing a custom backend handler that accepts base64 or binary streams. No `multer` is required. I\'ll need to analyze the file name for class generation, and perhaps use Gemini to generate the decompiled headers dynamically.\n\n\n**Designing Dynamic Analyzer**\n\nI\'ve got a breakthrough: I\'m leveraging Gemini to analyze the uploaded `.ipa` and dynamically generate metadata. The goal is no more hardcoded presets! This allows the chatbot to instantly adapt to any custom file and stay extremely relevant, fast, and smart. I\'m focusing on deriving a rich class dump, and I\'ll carefully consider layout and typography to reflect elite craftsmanship. I\'ll focus on a high-end dark theme with clean borders.\n\n\n"
