  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Model Selection**\n\nI\'m currently focused on incorporating a model selector for the AI chatbot. The key is to allow users to easily switch between different models like `gemini-3.5-flash` or `gemini-pro-latest`. I\'m prioritizing user-friendliness in this implementation to enhance the overall experience. The implementation of this feature is actively in progress.\n\n\n**Investigating Command Execution**\n\nI\'m now diving into the execution of commands, specifically focusing on why the `class-dump` commands aren\'t functioning as expected. It seems Gemini is currently hallucinating the output. I\'m considering several solutions. I\'m evaluating integrating a backend interpreter to simulate `grep` on the simulated file, or prompting Gemini for more authentic responses. I\'m also checking whether the UI is even displaying the output. I\'m also looking at the fact that the Model Selector is hardcoded in the back-end as well.\n\n\n**Analyzing Command Execution**\n\nI\'ve realized the current setup has the model hallucinating command outputs due to the single-turn nature of the UI. My new plan involves a server-side interception. If the bot suggests a command, or if we instruct the bot to send the command, we\'ll run it and return the result. This approach does create the need for two LLM calls. The bot currently responds with `{ commandRun: { command: \"...\", output: \"...\" } }`, and is not working as expected. I\'m focusing on the `/api/chat` route and considering a simulated grep on the backend.\n\n\n**Exploring Command Integration**\n\nI\'m now exploring command integration further. I\'m considering a \"ReAct\" pattern with two LLM calls, one for the command, the other for the response. However, since the LLM has the full text of `ipaMetadata`, perhaps the `grep` is just a stylistic output issue. I will check `TweakChat.tsx` to understand the `commandRun` rendering logic and identify any potential response schema violations. The simple model selector might allow for tweaking prompts for better `grep` format. I am also determining if the backend can run commands.\n\n\n**Refining Command Execution**\n\nI\'m now focused on executing the `grep` commands directly on the JSON data within `server.ts`. If the model requests a regex search, I will perform the action on the server-side, eliminating hallucinations. The `/api/chat` route will be updated to accept `modelName`, and I\'m adding the model selector to `TweakChat.tsx` so I can vary the model for each query. I\'m starting by adding the model select in the header of `TweakChat.tsx` using `gemini-3.5-flash` and `gemini-pro-latest` options.\n\n\n**Implementing Model Selection**\n\nI\'m implementing a model selection feature, updating `Chat.tsx` with a `<select>` for the model. I\'ve updated `TweakChatProps` and `App.tsx`\'s state to manage and pass the `aiModel`. The model selection is also going to be included in the POST body of `handleSendMessage` in `App.tsx`. I\'ve updated `server.ts` to use the model name when generating content.\n\nI\'m also fixing the hallucinated command outputs by making sure the format for `commandRun` is valid. I\'m focusing on ensuring it\'s an optional string array, so the UI can render it even if it\'s missing or badly formatted.\n\n\n**Debugging Command Outputs**\n\nI\'m now diagnosing the `commandRun` output failures. I\'ve updated the system prompt in `server.ts` to guide the model on producing simulated terminal output, and I\'m updating the response schema. I\'m checking why the `grep` results might not be visible to the user, potentially due to error handling falling back to a \"Failed to connect...\" message. I\'ll confirm how to make the optional `commandRun` work correctly.\n\n\n**Investigating Error Handling**\n\nI\'m now investigating server error handling to address hallucinated command outputs, especially with JSON-focused prompts. I\'m also planning to enhance the UI by integrating grep results directly within the chat. I\'m considering directly parsing user messages in `server.ts`. If the message starts with `grep` or `class-dump`, I can execute the command and return the output without involving Gemini, to get better output for the user. I need to make sure I am accounting for an object type with command and output fields.\n\n\n**Simulating Command Output**\n\nI\'m now focusing on improving how the AI handles `grep` commands. My current approach involves refining the system prompt to instruct Gemini to simulate terminal output using the provided `ipaMetadata`. If a user types a command like `grep Jailbreak`, the AI should generate the `commandRun.output` directly from the metadata. I\'m considering if the Gemini API\'s \"tools\" feature offers benefits. I\'m also planning to parse `ipaMetadata` internally for even greater control.\n\n\n**Enhancing Output Simulation**\n\nI\'m now refining the simulation of command outputs. I\'m focusing on `server.ts` to directly simulate `grep` functionality when the AI\'s requested output uses the metadata, simplifying prompt design. I\'m updating the system instruction to explicitly guide Gemini to use the provided metadata for command simulation and setting the default model. I\'m also ensuring the model is correctly passed from the frontend to the backend for accurate generation.\n\n\n**Refining Command Integration**\n\nI\'m now focusing on integrating simulated server-side command execution. My plan is to intercept user messages starting with `grep` or `class-dump` in `server.ts`. I will bypass Gemini entirely for these commands, directly parsing and executing them on `ipaMetadata` for speed and accuracy. This should provide users with reliable and instant results. I\'m also ensuring the model is correctly passed from the frontend to the backend for accurate generation. I\'m looking to add a literal server-side local search so that it is fast and accurate.\n\n\n**Simplifying Schema Response**\n\nI\'m now revising the JSON schema to improve command output reliability. I\'m making the `commandRun` fields completely optional to reduce schema compliance failures. I\'m also addressing potential issues with JSON limits to ensure longer tweak code isn\'t truncated or mishandled. Instead of removing the `responseSchema`, I am wrapping `JSON.parse` to gracefully handle malformed responses. I\'ll test how the different models like `gemini-3.5-flash` handle this change.\n\n\n**Improving UI Appearance**\n\nI\'m now focusing on UI improvements. I\'m streamlining `TweakChat`, removing unnecessary elements like the \"Generating...\" text and the \"Substrate Coach\" label. I am simplifying colors and borders, aiming for a cleaner, minimalist dark mode, and reducing unnecessary text. I\'ll remove the \"Veteran iOS Tweak Coach\" to just \"Interactive Patch Assistant\". I\'m making sure to address all UI improvements to look less \"HORRIBLE\".\n\n\n**Reviewing Application Initialization**\n\nI\'m now revisiting `App.tsx` and focusing on the application\'s initial state. I\'ve re-reviewed its contents, and the automatic loading of `YouTube.ipa` is gone. There\'s no longer a `useEffect` triggering a pre-loaded IPA. The previous changes successfully removed that behavior, so the app now starts cleaner. I also verified the `/api/analyze-ipa` endpoint uses the uploaded user file. I\'m double-checking for any lingering default templates in `server.ts` to ensure no mock data is accidentally used.\n\n\n**Refining Application Startup**\n\nI\'m now revisiting `App.tsx` again to ensure a clean startup. I\'ve confirmed the automatic loading of `YouTube.ipa` is removed, eliminating the need for a default template in the backend. I\'m focusing on cleaning up `App.tsx` to prevent any unexpected pre-loaded IPA behavior. I am streamlining the application\'s initialization, and making sure the initial application experience is clean, starting from the user uploaded file.\n\n\n\n**Optimizing UI Elements**\n\nI\'m now focusing on refining the UI across several components. I\'m removing the \"Sparkles\" icon, suggested patches, and neon gradients, transitioning to a clean, flat design with a dark mode. Specifically, I\'m making message bubbles flat, with a gray background for user messages and a transparent background with a border for bot messages. I\'m also ensuring the model selector is in the top right. I\'m also renaming \"Interactive Patch Assistant\". I\'ll be cleaning up the UI in `TweakEditor.tsx` and `IpaSelector.tsx`.\n\n\n\n**Enhancing Error Handling**\n\nI\'m now focusing on improving the server\'s error handling for malformed JSON responses from Gemini. I\'m adding `gemini-1.5-pro` to the model selection, and addressing JSON parsing errors using a more robust approach. I\'m extracting the JSON from the LLM text by cleaning up the response and ensuring the appropriate model is available to the application. I\'ll clean up the UI and streamline the chat interface, and I\'ll modify the system prompt to explicitly inform Gemini of the user upload.\n\n\n\n**Refining App Cleanup**\n\nI\'m now streamlining the application\'s visual presentation and behavior within `App.tsx` and `server.ts`. I\'m focused on removing the \"TweakStudio\" header in `App.tsx` and the flame icon, aiming for a cleaner, more professional interface. In `server.ts`, I\'m carefully reviewing the system prompt to explicitly reference the custom file, and adjusting the instructions to focus on clear, clean command output, and remove any references to YouTube or Spotify unless they are the target applications, which should be in the `ipaMetadata`. I\'m modifying `server.ts` to ensure the metadata is correctly handled.\n\n\n**Improving Command Generation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the server-side logic for command generation. I\'ve updated the system prompt to instruct Gemini to generate realistic terminal output based solely on the provided metadata. I\'m verifying the backend properly simulates `grep` command e